---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = "Beijing Travel Guide: What to See, Eat, and Know";
const description = "Your complete Beijing guide — attractions, transport, food, and where to stay.";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} lang="en">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">🏙️ Beijing</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">Beijing Travel Guide: What to See, Eat, and Know</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">Your complete Beijing guide — attractions, transport, food, and where to stay.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: June 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 8 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-50 to-orange-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-900 mb-4">⚡ Quick Answer</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-red-800 leading-relaxed">Beijing is <strong>China's political and cultural heart</strong> — home to the Forbidden City, the Great Wall, and some of the best Peking duck you'll ever eat. It's a city of contrasts where ancient alleyways (hutongs) sit alongside modern skyscrapers. Beijing is also the most convenient entry point for first-time visitors, with two international airports and the largest high-speed rail hub in the country.</p>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">📋 In This Guide</h3>
      <ul class="space-y-3">
        <li><a href="#why-visit" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏛️ Why Visit Beijing?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-many-days" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#attractions" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🎯 Top Attractions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#getting-around" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🚇 Getting Around</a></li>
        <li><a href="#where-to-stay" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🏨 Where to Stay</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-to-eat" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">🍜 What to Eat</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="why-visit" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🏛️ Why Visit Beijing?</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Beijing isn't just a city — it's a living museum of Chinese civilization. Walking through the Forbidden City, you're following the footsteps of 24 emperors. Climbing the Great Wall, you're standing on a structure built over 2,000 years. But Beijing is also a vibrant modern capital with incredible food, world-class museums, and a nightlife scene that never sleeps.</p>

    <h2 id="how-many-days" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">📅 How Many Days Do You Need?</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-4"><strong>3 days is the minimum</strong> for the essential Beijing experience.</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6">
      <li><strong>3 days (standard):</strong> Forbidden City + Great Wall + a taste of hutongs and Temple of Heaven</li>
      <li><strong>4–5 days (comfortable):</strong> Add the Summer Palace, 798 Art District, and a food tour</li>
      <li><strong>5+ days (deep dive):</strong> Day trip to Chengde Mountain Resort or explore the city's lesser-known temples</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">The Great Wall alone takes a full day — it's about 1.5 hours by car to the nearest section (Mutianyu), so plan accordingly.</p>

    <!-- ==================== ATTRACTIONS ==================== -->
    <h2 id="attractions" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🎯 Top Attractions</h2>

    <!-- Forbidden City -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/forbidden-city.webp" alt="Forbidden City palace complex in Beijing" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Forbidden City (故宫)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">The world's largest palace complex — and it feels even bigger when you're standing in it. The complex spans 720,000 square meters with nearly 1,000 buildings, all gold-trimmed roofs and red walls that haven't faded in 600 years. You'll walk on the same stones as 24 emperors, through courtyards so vast they make you feel small. Book tickets at least a week ahead — only 80,000 people get in each day, and it's closed every Monday.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Mutianyu Great Wall -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/great-wall.webp" alt="Mutianyu Great Wall winding across mountain ridges" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Mutianyu Great Wall (慕田峪长城)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">The best-preserved section of the Great Wall, and the only one with a toboggan ride down. It stretches over 2,000 meters across 23 Ming-dynasty watchtowers, restored to their original condition. From the wall, you see forest-covered ridges rolling in every direction — no souvenir shops, no modern buildings, just mountains. Take the cable car up and the toboggan down; the whole thing is worth the 90-minute drive from town.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Temple of Heaven -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/temple-of-heaven.webp" alt="Temple of Heaven historic Ming dynasty temple in Beijing" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Temple of Heaven (天坛)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">A perfect circle of blue-glazed tiles rising against the sky — the Temple of Heaven is where Ming and Qing emperors came to pray for good harvests. The Hall of Prayer for Good Harvests stands 38 meters tall, built entirely without a single nail. In the surrounding park you'll hear opera singers warming up, birdsong, and the clack of mahjong tiles before 7 AM. Go at dawn to see Beijing at its most local.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Summer Palace -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/summer-palace.webp" alt="Summer Palace imperial garden with Kunming Lake in Beijing" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Summer Palace (颐和园)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">An imperial escape so grand it makes other royal gardens look like courtyards — the Dowager Empress Cixi spent the navy's budget on this place. Kunming Lake alone covers 220 hectares, crossed by a 17-arch bridge that glows gold at sunset. Walk the shaded Long Corridor — 728 meters of painted ceilings with over 14,000 scenes from Chinese legend. Come in late autumn for golden ginkgo trees and thin crowds.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Jingshan Park -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/jingshan-park-v2.webp" alt="Jingshan Park panoramic view over the Forbidden City rooftops at sunset" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Jingshan Park (景山公园)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">The single best view of the Forbidden City — and it's right across the street from the palace's north gate. Jingshan is an artificial hill built from the earth excavated during the Forbidden City's construction, so it rises directly above the palace rooftops. At sunset, the golden light hits the glazed tiles and the whole complex glows beneath you like a sea of red and gold. The climb takes five minutes. Go at 5 PM, grab a spot at the Wanchun Pavilion, and wait for the light to turn. This is the photo every Beijing visitor should have.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Lama Temple -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/lama-temple-v2.webp" alt="Lama Temple incense smoke rising before ornate red and gold hall facade" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Lama Temple (雍和宫)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">The most important Tibetan Buddhist temple outside Tibet — and a place where the line between tourism and devotion gets thin. The main hall houses a 26-meter sandalwood Buddha carved from a single tree, the tallest of its kind in the world. But what makes the Lama Temple feel alive is the incense: thick coils of sandalwood smoke drift through the courtyards, and locals queue with burning sticks to press their palms to the prayer wheels. If you're inclined, light a stick and make a wish — the temple has a reputation for answering prayers, and plenty of Beijing locals swear by it. Just don't photograph the worshippers up close. It is a working temple, not a museum.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Hutongs -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-start bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <div class="w-full rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <img src="/images/beijing/hutongs.webp" alt="Beijing Hutongs traditional narrow alleyways" class="w-full h-auto object-cover" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Hutongs (胡同)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700">Beijing's ancient alleyways that survived the wrecking ball — a network of narrow lanes where life hasn't changed much in a century. Some hutongs date back over 700 years to the Yuan dynasty. You'll see old men playing chess on stools, bicycles propped against grey brick walls, and laundry hanging between power lines. Skip the tourist-packed Nanluoguxiang and walk Wudaoying Hutong or the lanes around Shichahai instead.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="getting-around" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🚇 Getting Around</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-lg text-gray-700 mb-12">
      <li><strong>Subway:</strong> Beijing's metro is extensive, cheap, and has English signs. It covers almost every major attraction. See <a href="/en/transport/metro-bus/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">Metro & Bus Guide for Major Chinese Cities</a> for tips on using the Beijing subway system.</li>
      <li><strong>Didi (China's Uber):</strong> Super convenient for door-to-door trips. Download the app before you arrive. You can enter destinations in English. Learn how to set it up in <a href="/en/transport/didi/" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">How to Use Didi in China as a Foreigner</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Taxis:</strong> Available, but communication can be challenging. Always show your destination in Chinese characters (have your hotel's name written in Chinese).</li>
      <li><strong>Biking:</strong> Beijing is surprisingly bikeable. Shared bikes (Hellobike, Meituan) are everywhere. Just use Alipay or WeChat to scan and ride.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="where-to-stay" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🏨 Where to Stay</h2>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Dongcheng District (东城区) — Best for first-time visitors</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">Central location near the Forbidden City, hutongs, and Wangfujing. Good balance of price and convenience.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Xicheng District (西城区) — Best for culture lovers</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">The quiet, traditional side. Near the Houhai lake area, Drum Tower, and many well-preserved hutongs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-12">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Chaoyang District (朝阳区) — Best for business travelers</h3>
      <p class="text-gray-700">The modern CBD. Many international hotels and Western restaurants. Near the 798 Art District and Sanlitun nightlife.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ==================== WHAT TO EAT ==================== -->
    <h2 id="what-to-eat" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-12">🍜 What to Eat</h2>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Beijing's food scene is built around bold flavors, wheat-based dishes, and imperial culinary traditions.</p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-12">
      <!-- Peking Duck -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/peking-duck.webp" alt="Peking Duck crispy skin wrapped in pancakes" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Peking Duck (北京烤鸭)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">A whole duck roasted until the skin shatters like glass — that's Peking Duck. The skin is crisp and fatty, the meat tender, wrapped in paper-thin pancakes with sweet bean sauce, cucumber strips, and scallions. Some locals dip the crispy skin in white sugar before wrapping it up. The restaurants around Qianmen have been doing it the old-school way for generations.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Zha Jiang Mian -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/zha-jiang-mian.webp" alt="Zha Jiang Mian noodles with soybean paste sauce" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Zha Jiang Mian (炸酱面)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Thick hand-pulled noodles buried under a dark sauce made from fermented soybeans and minced pork. The sauce is salty and deep, with a slight bitterness from the bean paste, cut by fresh cucumber and bean sprout toppings. Mix everything together until every strand is coated before you take a bite. Find the best bowls in the old hutongs near Dongsi.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Jianbing -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/jianbing.webp" alt="Jianbing savory Chinese crepe with egg and scallions" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Jianbing (煎饼)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Beijing's breakfast on the go — a thin millet crepe spread on a hot griddle, cracked with an egg, and folded around a crunchy wonton cracker. The contrast is everything: soft crepe, crispy center, spicy chili sauce, and fresh scallions cutting through the richness. Eat it straight out of the foil wrapper while it's still hot. Grab one from a street cart near any subway station before 9 AM — they're usually gone by noon.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Lamb Hotpot -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/lamb-hotpot.webp" alt="Lamb Hotpot shuan yang rou copper pot boiling broth" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Lamb Hotpot — Shuan Yang Rou (涮羊肉)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">A copper chimney pot filled with bubbling broth — this is the northern Chinese way of eating hotpot, and it's all about the lamb. The meat is sliced tissue-thin so it cooks in seconds, staying tender with a clean, gamey flavor. Swirl it in the boiling broth for no more than ten seconds, then drag it through sesame sauce with fermented tofu. The small family-run spots near Gulou are packed with locals on winter nights.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Douzhi -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/douzhi.webp" alt="Douzhi fermented mung bean drink Beijing local delicacy" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Douzhi (豆汁)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">The drink that divides Beijing locals from everyone else — fermented mung bean juice that smells like old vegetables and tastes sour and earthy. It's served warm, with a gray-green hue and a slightly chunky texture, always alongside a salty sesame ring and pickled radish. You don't sip it, you gulp it. Order a bowl at the breakfast shops in the old hutongs near Tiantan and see if you're one of the few out-of-towners who actually likes it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Sugar-coated Hawthorn -->
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center bg-gray-50 p-3">
          <img src="/images/beijing/bingtanghulu.webp" alt="Sugar-coated Hawthorn candied hawthorn berries on a stick" class="max-w-full max-h-56 object-contain" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="p-4">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Sugar-coated Hawthorn (冰糖葫芦)</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 text-base">Candied hawthorn berries threaded on a bamboo stick and coated in a hard sugar shell. The first bite cracks the sugar, and then you hit the sourness of the berry — that sweet-and-sour rush is what makes it addictive. The sugar coating is thin enough to shatter, thick enough to keep the berries from tasting too tart. Vendors sell them on sticks from carts all over Wangfujing night market and the hutongs year-round.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose mt-16">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">📚 Related Guides</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="/en/cities/city-select/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🗺️ <strong>How Many Days in China?</strong> — Plan your full itinerary</a>
        <a href="/en/cities/shanghai-guide/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🌆 <strong>Shanghai Travel Guide</strong> — Compare with China's other great city</a>
        <a href="/en/transport/transport-guide/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🚄 <strong>How to Get Around China</strong> — All your transport options</a>
        <a href="/en/transport/high-speed-rail/" class="block bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-lg hover:border-blue-300 transition">🚄 <strong>How to Book and Ride China High-Speed Rail</strong> — Beijing to Shanghai by train</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
